{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The generalized probe executed green and closes the core finding's scope limit with measurement rather than argument. Receipt 9f3f25cc1783466ab2a5b0b6be9aa7d7 (experiment 224b9ea47a35472db872f526b5cf4392-experiment, exit 0, three cases passed, no parse loss) carries both sizes in one run: at three nodes (`three_node_lost_accepteds_window`: produced BLE:2025/Prepare:2/Promise:2/AcceptSync:2/Accepted:4/AcceptDecide:2, dropped 2 Accepted receipts, accept_decide_total 2, leader_decided_idx 0, both followers accepted_idx 1 and holding the entry Undecided) the leader stayed undecided for 100 resend periods once both receipts the quorum still needed were gone, and `three_node_later_proposal_repairs` shows the later unrelated proposal deciding both entries in 1 step; at two nodes the same run repeats the earlier picture (dropped Accepted:1, after_drop only BLE:667, leader_decided_idx 0) with its delivered control deciding in 1 step. The account now states the fault assumption explicitly — one lost receipt at two nodes, two at three, i.e. exactly the quorum's completion — and replaces the old 'only 2-node probed' limit with that scoped assumption plus three others (no clusters beyond three nodes; client retry can repair as a side effect; a ballot change re-advertises acceptance through handle_acceptsync and is deliberately kept out by holding leadership stable). I also recorded one observation I cannot explain rather than smoothing it over: the three-node case produced four Accepted messages and `after_drop` still shows an Accepted crossing the wire while the leader did not decide — flagged as an open question about the probe's message accounting, not as target behaviour.\"}"}}
